  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nSaturday-Sunday:\nHigh pressure settles over the region into Saturday, with clouds\nclearing the area. Expect highs in the upper 50s to lower 60s on\nSaturday afternoon, with light northeast winds. Clear skies and\ncalm winds set the stage for another chilly night, including\nincreasing chances for areas of frost. The latest guidance\nsuggests probabilities of frost formation (< 35 degrees as a\nproxy) have increased to 60-90% in areas, especially across the\neastern Ozarks. It is even reasonable to expect the much lower \nelevations and protected areas out east will fall below \nfreezing. It is fairly common for this area to see temperatures \n5+ degrees below surrounding observations. Elsewhere, patchy \nfrost remains in the cards for Sunday morning and potential \nheadlines will be assessed with future forecast updates. For \nsome context, the average first frost date for the eastern \nOzarks ranges from October 10-15, while the average first freeze\ndata ranges from October 17-22. So while we have had quite warm\ntemperatures September and the first half of October, the first\nfrost/freeze is actually coming around right on time in terms \nof climatology. Highs a bit warmer into Sunday, reaching into \nthe middle to upper 60s.\n\nEarly Next Week:\nA ridge gradually amplifies over the central CONUS into \nearly/mid next week, supporting a return of above normal \ntemperatures. This will be characterized by high temperatures \nback into the middle to upper 70s, to even lower 80s by mid-\nweek. This aligns with the latest CPC forecasts, depicting \n70-80% chances for above normal temperatures into mid to late \nnext week. Meanwhile, any rain chances remain low (< 10%) \nthrough early next week.",
